**1. `src/domain/work/run-report.ts`** (pure):

```ts
/**
 * run-report.ts — which runner runs owe the thread an ending, read from the outside.
 * ...
 */
export interface RunTitle { agent: string; number: number; }

export function parseRunTitle(displayTitle: string | undefined): RunTitle | undefined {
  const match = /^(\S+) on #(\d+)$/.exec((displayTitle ?? "").trim());
  if (!match) return undefined;
  return { agent: match[1]!, number: Number(match[2]) };
}

export interface DatedComment { body: string; createdAt: string; }

/** The thread's newest turn-ending comment, quotations skipped. */
export function lastEndingComment(comments: readonly DatedComment[]): DatedComment | undefined {
  for (let i = comments.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const c = comments[i]!;
    if (isQuotation(c.body)) continue;       // ← adapters import in domain? NO — layering!
    ...
  }
}
```

PROBLEM: `isQuotation` and `ENDED_TAG` are in adapters/github/tags.ts; domain must not import adapters (layering: adapters import domain — check layering.test.ts constraint!). tests/contract/layering.test.ts — presumably enforces direction. So the domain module can't read ENDED_TAG. Solution: domain/work/run-report.ts takes the READER as a parameter — the pattern `TurnReaders` already established (domain defines the interface, adapters supply the readers)!

So:

```ts
export interface EndingReaders {
  /** How a body ended a turn, or "" — the same shape domain/work/thread.ts's TurnReaders.ending. */
  ending: (body: string) => string;   // hmm — typed as the tag value or ""?
  /** Whether a body quotes another comment (the guard's notice), whose tags are not the thread's own. */
  quoted: (body: string) => boolean;
}
```

Hmm — wait, but thread.ts's TurnReaders.ending returns "returned"|"handed-off"|"waiting"|"" — the EVENT vocabulary, which loses stopped/limit/done distinctions (fine for me: I need "is an ending" + whose-turn). For needsEnding I need "is this body an ending at all" — event != "" ✓. For the label rule I need the event → whoseTurn ✓. So I can reuse `readers` (TurnReaders) from adapters/github/thread.ts as THE reader the script passes in, and the domain function accepts `TurnReaders`!

Design:

```ts
// domain/work/run-report.ts
import type { TurnReaders } from "./thread.ts";

export interface RunTitle { agent: string; number: number; }
export function parseRunTitle(displayTitle: string | undefined): RunTitle | undefined;

export interface DatedComment { readonly body: string; readonly createdAt: string; }

/** Newest comment that is a turn-ending (quotations skipped), or undefined. */
export function lastEndingComment(comments: readonly DatedComment[], readers: TurnReaders): DatedComment | undefined;

/** True when the run left the thread without an ending: no ending at/after runStartedAt. */
export function needsEnding(comments: readonly DatedComment[], runStartedAt: string | undefined, readers: TurnReaders): boolean;
```

Hmm — wait: needsEnding = "no ending comment ≥ runStartedAt" — computed from lastEndingComment: if none → true; if last.createdAt parses and >= started → false; if last < started → true; if started unparseable/missing → true (conservative); if last.createdAt unparseable → hmm — an ending we cannot place in time: count it or not? Conservative = notify (treat as not counting) — but that could double-notify... conservative in which direction? Double-notify (noise, harmless) vs silence-when-stuck (the jam persists — the harm this issue fixes). Notify side ✓. And document.

Timestamp comparison: Date.parse both sides; compare epoch ms with `>=` (inclusive per issue's "created_at 以降").

parseRunTitle details: the run-name is `"${{ inputs.agent }} on #${{ inputs.number }}"`. Regex `^(\S+) on #(\d+)$`. Trim the input. Also tolerate number 0? `#\d+` with 0 → number 0 → an issue 0 doesn't exist; the thread read would fail → red watch → visible. Meh — accept \d+ and let the read fail loudly. Hmm — or reject 0 as unparseable? GitHub numbers start at 1. `(\d+)` then `if number === 0 return undefined`? Keep simple: accept; the read failure is loud. Hmm — actually a number that can't exist → the watch goes red for EVERY old... no — old titles don't parse the regex at all. 0 only if number input were "0" — impossible in practice. Keep `>= 1` check anyway — cheap, and "unparseable → silent" is the safe direction. Hmm, wait — is it? A malformed title for a REAL run → silent → jam persists (the hole this closes). A malformed title from an UNRELATED run → must be silent. The tension: I can't tell "unrelated" from "malformed runner title". The runner's title format is fully ours; anything not matching it IS unrelated-or-old ✓ silent is right.

**2. `recoveryLines(agent)` in comment-command.ts** — the two continue lines, shared:

```ts
/**
 * The two ways a person restarts work after a run that ended, as the failure notices say them.
 * ...
 */
export function recoveryLines(agent: string): string[] {
  return [
    `- Continue: comment \`/${agent}\` and put what to do next on the following lines.`,
    `- Start clean: comment \`/${agent} recover\`, which archives this session and rebuilds`,
    "  it from the issue.",
  ];
}
```

And refactor report_run_failure.ts's failureNotice to use it (same lines verbatim ✓ — diff-clean).

